From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 11:45:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] indexeddb: Answer the manager's metadata reads from the
 committed metadata

Since each database's work runs on its one connection, in order, the
manager's synchronous metadata reads waited behind the work handed over
before: the object store parameters of a transaction's first
objectStore(), the version at open and in databases(). A readonly
getAll over 50,000 records held the script thread that called
objectStore(), and every IndexedDB request of the process, for about
2 s on the DAYU200.

The engine now keeps the version and the object stores (names, key
paths, whether they have a key generator, indexes) as of the last
commit. It loads them when it opens the database, and an upgrade
transaction reloads them inside its SQL transaction right before
COMMIT and publishes them once COMMIT has succeeded; if the reload
fails, the commit fails. Only upgrade transactions change the
metadata, and the manager only needs it as committed: script keeps
the object stores that an upgrade creates or renames, and asks the
backend only about stores the upgrade hasn't touched. The reads
answer at once, and no longer run statements inside a writer's SQL
transaction.

databases() also filters by origin before it reads versions, and now
reads committed versions, as the spec asks.

Tests: the metadata reads answer while a job holds the connection; the
version is the committed one after an upgrade without requests commits
and after one aborts; inside an open upgrade transaction the SQL state
has the upgrade's changes while the reads report the committed state,
and both report the old state after the abort.

Validation: host `cargo nextest run -p servo-storage -p servo-storage-traits`: 105 passed, 1 skipped, as before (the manager-read test of the transaction model is replaced by one for the metadata reads). DAYU200 at the tip of the series, `./mach test-unit --ohos -p servo-storage -- [--no-default-features] --features ohos-rdb-backend --status-level all`: RDB only 106 passed, 3 skipped; both backends 182 passed, 4 skipped (the skips are the documented test_new_storage_threads_create_independent_groups and the ignored long tests). A temporary probe page (50,000 records of 100 bytes; during a readonly getAll, objectStore() on another store in the same task, and an open of an unrelated database), 3 runs each: on the DAYU200 (QuickJS + RDB) objectStore() took 1,932 to 2,019 ms and the open 1,936 to 2,022 ms before, 0 ms and 5 ms now, with the getAll at 2.7 s both times; host SpiderMonkey 5 ms and 7 to 8 ms before, 0 ms and 2 ms now. WPT IndexedDB/ at the tip of the series (with the P1 review fixes), passing subtests of 4,511: host SpiderMonkey 3,251 with no unexpected result, host QuickJS 3,253, DAYU200 in the board configuration (QuickJS + RDB) 3,247 (before the series 3,224, 3,229 and 3,222), and /_mozilla/IndexedDB/ 46 of 46 on all three; no new failures: QuickJS idbversionchangeevent.any.sharedworker timed out once and passed 20 of 20 on both host engines, and the board's idbversionchangeevent.any.worker saw a blocked event from the sharedworker variant, which uses the same database name and whose connection deleteDatabase doesn't close yet; the other differences are known flips. The SpiderMonkey runs used this tree with an earlier form of the databases() change, rewritten since with the same behaviour. `./mach check --ohos -- -p servo-storage --all-targets` warning-free with SQLite only, RDB only and both. CI-parity checks at this tree: `./mach clippy --locked -- -- --deny warnings`, `RUSTFLAGS="-D warnings" ./mach build --use-crown --locked --profile checked-release`, the same OHOS build for the default and harmonyos flavors, each leaving a signed HAP, and `./mach test-unit --profile checked-release` (1,308 passed, 1 skipped) and `--doc` pass.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; the agent wrote the change and its tests, and measured the stall on the host and the DAYU200, from the P1 review's finding)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
